        "content": "## Problem Statement\n**Exercise 1**: Given the function  \n\\[\nf(x) = x^2 + 2x + 1,\n\\]\nfind:\n\n1. The *derivative* \\(f'(x)\\)\n2. The **integral** \\(\\int f(x)\\,dx\\)\n\n### Solution\n\nThe derivative is:\n\\[\nf'(x) = 2x + 2\n\\]\n\nThe integral is:\n\\[\n\\int f(x)\\,dx = \\frac{x^3}{3} + x^2 + x + C\n\\]\n\n---\n\n## Linear Algebra with Lists\nProperties of the matrix  \n\\[\nA = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix}:\n\\]\n\n- Determinant: \\(\\det(A) = ad - bc\\)\n- Trace: \\(\\operatorname{tr}(A) = a + d\\)\n- Inverse (if it exists):\n  \\[\n  A^{-1} = \\frac{1}{ad - bc}\n  \\begin{pmatrix}\n  d & -b \\\\\n  -c & a\n  \\end{pmatrix}\n  \\quad \\text{for } ad-bc \\ne 0\n  \\]\n\n---\n\n## Real World Context\n> **Note**: In economics, the cost function \\(C(q) = 100 + 5q\\) means fixed costs are \\(\\$100\\), and variable costs are \\(\\$5\\) per unit.\n\nMoney context: If you buy \\(10\\) units, total cost is\n\\[\nC(10) = 100 + 5(10) = 150,\n\\]\nso that is \\(\\$100\\) fixed plus \\(\\$50\\) variable \\(=\\$150\\) total.",
